+# M4 plan · Platter core (2026-10-19, written 2026-10-21)
+
+Goal (spec §5 M4): the pure platter core in `mapping/rane-one-platter.js`: decoder, speed estimator, state machine
+and the hybrid switch-over decision, plus `tools/synth-platter` to feed them labelled data. This plan fixes the API,
+the constants, every transition and the switch-over rule, so M4-03..M4-07 only build and measure.
+
+The spec calls M4 the "hypothesis edition". It isn't quite: `captures/2026-10-19-quick/platter.jsonl` holds real
+platter data from one deck, analysed in M4-01 (`docs/research/2026-10-19-quick-capture-analysis.md`, decision D19).
+The core follows that capture where it contradicts the Twelve/V7 hypothesis (prior art §2), and says so. Everything
+that one capture can't show is marked UNVERIFIED here and must be marked the same way in the code. New defaults
+from this plan are **D20 *default*** in `docs/decisions.md`. The numbers that aren't in the analysis document come
+from short Node one-liners over the same capture, run while writing this plan. Their results are under Real-capture
+check, and M4-07 re-measures them with the core itself.
+
+Build-only: everything here is pure code tested in Node. No MIDI is sent and no Mixxx runs in M4.
+
+## Loading
+
+- `mapping/rane-one-platter.js` is a **plain script**. It has no `import`, `export`, `require` or `module`, and
+  doesn't use the identifiers `engine`, `midi` or `print`, not even in comments, because M4-03's purity test greps
+  for them. It defines exactly one global:
+
+  ```js
+  var RaneOnePlatter = (function() {
+      "use strict";
+      // ... constants, helpers ...
+      return { DEFAULTS, createDecoder, createSpeedEstimator, createStateMachine };
+  })();
+  ```
+
+  It has to be `var`, not `const`. A top-level `var` becomes a property of the global object both in Mixxx's
+  QJSEngine and in a Node `vm` context, but a top-level `const` does neither.
+- **Mixxx** loads it first (`Rane-One.midi.xml` already lists `rane-one-platter.js` with `functionprefix=""` before
+  `Rane-One-scripts.js`). The glue reads `RaneOnePlatter`. The language level is what Mixxx 2.5's QJSEngine (Qt 6)
+  accepts: `const`/`let`, arrow functions, template strings, `Object.freeze`. No classes with private fields, no
+  optional chaining (keep to what Mixxx's ESLint config allows).
+- **Node** loads it with `tests/helpers/load-platter.mjs` (M4-03):
+
+  ```js
+  import fs from "node:fs"; import vm from "node:vm";
+  export function loadPlatter() {
+      const src = fs.readFileSync(new URL("../../mapping/rane-one-platter.js", import.meta.url), "utf8");
+      const context = {};                       // no engine, midi, print, console, require
+      vm.runInNewContext(src, context, { filename: "rane-one-platter.js" });
+      return context.RaneOnePlatter;
+  }
+  ```
+
+  With no `engine`, `midi` or `print` in the context, any stray call throws a ReferenceError in a test.
+  `tests/platter-pure.test.mjs` also greps the source for `\b(engine|midi|print|require|import)\b`.
+- **Factories, no shared state.** Each `create*` returns a fresh object holding its own state, so two decks get two
+  decoders, and tests never leak state into each other. `config` is optional. Missing keys come from `DEFAULTS`,
+  and unknown keys are ignored. The factories copy what they need and never mutate `config`.
+- **Never throws.** Every public function catches internally. Malformed input returns `null` (`feed`) or the
+  previous result (`push`, `update`), and the factory counts it in `stats().malformed`. Each object has a
+  `stats()` method that returns a plain object of counters. The glue's fallback (CLAUDE.md: play at the pitch
+  fader's rate) is M7's job. The core never forces the clock on its own.
+
+## Constants
+
+All of them live in `RaneOnePlatter.DEFAULTS` (frozen). Any of them can be overridden through `config`, and the
+source header lists each with the citation or UNVERIFIED given here (M4-08 step 2). "Capture" below means
+`captures/2026-10-19-quick/platter.jsonl`, and phases and numbers are from the analysis document unless marked
+"plan one-liner".
+
+| Name | Default | Source |
+|---|---|---|
+| `TICKS_PER_REV` | 3600 | Capture 20–36 s: 31,984 ticks in 16 s, 3598.2 per rev **if** the motor ran at exactly 33⅓. 3600 vs 3584 (28 × 128, a motor 0.4 % fast) is **UNVERIFIED** until a mark-to-mark count (spec §6 step 3, M5). |
+| `TIMER_HZ` | 2822400 | Capture 20–36 s: 2,822,469 timer counts per host second (+0.002 %, needs no rpm). |
+| `TICK_MOD` | 128 | Capture: CC 6 is a 7-bit running counter that wraps 127 → 0 (D19). |
+| `TIMER_MOD` | 16384 | Capture: the pitch bend is a 14-bit timestamp (values 0–16383), rising even when ticks fall (9–15 s). |
+| `RPM_33` | 100/3 | Capture 20–36 s is consistent with 33⅓ at 3600 ticks/rev (−0.05 %). That the One's "33" is exactly 100/3 is **UNVERIFIED** (no reference clock in the capture). |
+| `RPM_45` | 45 | **UNVERIFIED**: no 45 rpm capture yet (spec §6 step 4). |
+| ~~`MIN_TIMED_SPEED_RATIO`~~ | removed in M4-05 (D23); was **0.1** (step 2 of M4-02 says 0.05) | Capture: no timer delta above 16,304 (5.777 ms). Longer gaps arrive as −1/−2, so the timer can time a tick only above TIMER_HZ / TIMER_MOD = 172.3 ticks/s, which is **0.086 of 33⅓** (0.064 of 45) at one tick per message. 0.05 is under that floor, where every timer value is a marker. 0.1 gives a 16 % margin. The margin itself is **UNVERIFIED** (D20). |
+| `TIMER_MARKER_MIN` | 16382 | Capture: unsigned deltas 16383 (−1, 274×) and 16382 (−2, 7×) are saturation markers, "5.8 ms or more" (hypothesis, **UNVERIFIED**, M5 check 2). |
+| `PAIR_WINDOW_US` | 2000 | Capture: every CC 6 is directly followed by its pitch bend. 29,248 of 29,323 pairs fall in the same host millisecond and the other 75 are 1 ms apart. In Mixxx both arrive in the same 5 ms poll. |
+| `HOST_WINDOW_MS` | 200 | Plan one-liner: the hold phase gives ≤ 0.0225 and the stopped tail ≤ 0.018 over 200 ms windows, while the ±20 % target at 0.02× needs ≥ 8 ticks per window (M4-05). Tuning, **UNVERIFIED**. |
+| `TIMER_GAP_MAX_US` | 20000 | Sanity limit: a paired sample more than 20 ms of host time after the previous one is not trusted for timing, because host-side message loss could hide a wrap. Plan one-liner: the largest host gap before a non-marker delta in the capture is 11 ms (37.716 s, device 4.28 ms). Host jitter puts any tighter limit at risk. Plan choice, **UNVERIFIED**. |
+| `TAKEOVER_TOL` | 0.03 | M4-06 step 2 (enter platter control at ±3 %). Plan one-liner: the timed per-message ratio in 20–36 s stays within 0.9945–1.0041. |
+| `HANDBACK_TOL` | 0.01 | M4-06 step 2 (back to the clock within ±1 %). Plan one-liner: the timed 50 ms windows in 20–36 s stay within ±0.31 %. |
+| `TAKEOVER_MS` | 10 | The "hold time" of M4-06 step 1: how long a deviation must last before control leaves the clock. Plan choice, **UNVERIFIED**, tuned against M4-07's glitch count. |
+| `SETTLE_MS` | 150 | How long the ratio must stay within `HANDBACK_TOL` before handback. Plan one-liner: after the 41 s release the ratio was back at 1.0 within 0.1 s. **UNVERIFIED**. |
+| `SPINUP_MS` | 1000 | How long after `expectedRatio` rises from 0 a slow platter still counts as spinning up. Capture: at speed ≈ 0.4 s after the 19.236 s press. **UNVERIFIED** for torque HIGH/LOW. |
+| `HELD_RATIO` / `HELD_EXIT_RATIO` | 0.1 / 0.2 | Held still means \|ratio\| ≤ 0.1, and it takes 0.2 to count as moving again. Plan one-liner: the hold phase (38.5–40.5 s) stays ≤ 0.0225 over 200 ms windows. |
+| `BACKSPIN_RATIO` / `BACKSPIN_EXIT_RATIO` | −0.5 / −0.4 | M4-06 step 1 (any → backspin below −0.5). The exit hysteresis is a plan choice. No backspin in the capture: **UNVERIFIED**. |
+| `STALL_MS` | 50 | How long the estimator holds its last ratio through heartbeats with no real sample (a host-side MIDI stall). A held record keeps sending about 16 samples/s (capture, 38–41 s), so a hand stop never relies on silence. Plan choice, **UNVERIFIED** against Mixxx's real delivery gaps (M7). |
+| `HELD_START_MS` | 150 | T5a: \|ratio\| ≤ `HELD_RATIO` this long after Play means the record is held, not spinning up. Plan one-liner: a free spin-up passes 0.10 within 50 ms. **UNVERIFIED** at torque LOW. |
+| `STOPPED_RATIO` | 0.03 | Plan one-liner: after the spin-down (from 45.7 s) the motor-off platter wanders by up to 0.018 over 200 ms windows. **UNVERIFIED** whether that is real motion or encoder noise (M5 check 3). |
+| `STOP_MS` | 250 | How long \|ratio\| ≤ `STOPPED_RATIO` must last for `stopped`. Plan choice. |
+| `MOVE_RATIO` | 0.05 | With the motor off, \|ratio\| above this leaves `stopped`. Above the 0.018 wander, and the slow hand turns (2–8 s, 9–15 s) run at 0.1–0.45. |
+| `RETARGET_MS` | 0 (off) | After an `expectedRatio` change, how long the at-speed band also covers the old value. Use it only if the motor turns out to follow the pitch fader with lag (spec §6 step 5, **UNVERIFIED**). |
+
+Derived values. They aren't configured separately, but each estimator and state machine computes them from its config:
+
+- `NOMINAL_TICKS_PER_S = TICKS_PER_REV · rpm / 60`: 2000 at 33⅓ and 2700 at 45, where `rpm` is `config.rpm`
+  (default `RPM_33`).
+- `TIMER_PER_TICK = TIMER_HZ / NOMINAL_TICKS_PER_S`: 1411.2 counts per tick at ratio 1.0 and 33⅓. The capture
+  measured 1411.9.
+
+## Decoder
+
+`createDecoder(config)` returns `{ feed(status, d1, d2, hostTimeUs), flush(hostTimeUs), pending(), stats() }`.
+
+- **Inputs:** raw MIDI bytes as Mixxx hands them to a script handler (`status`, `d1`, `d2`), plus the host time in
+  µs. Mixxx gives scripts no host timestamp, so in M7 the glue passes `Date.now() * 1000`. In Node, tools pass the
+  capture's `t_us`. The decoder listens to one deck. `config.ccStatus` (default `0xB0`), `config.cc` (default 6)
+  and `config.pbStatus` (default `0xE0`) select it. These are deck 1 in the capture. Deck 2's channel is
+  **UNVERIFIED** (spec §6 step 3).
+- **Returns** `null` or one sample `{ ticks, timer, hostTimeUs }`:
+  - `ticks`: the cumulative unwrapped tick count since the first CC, an integer. Each CC adds
+    `signed((d2 − prevD2) mod TICK_MOD)` in [−64, 63]. So 127 → 0 adds +1, 0 → 127 adds −1, 126 → 1 adds +3 and
+    1 → 126 adds −3. One message carries up to 63 ticks, which is 63,000 ticks/s at the 1 kHz message cap (31×);
+    the capture's maximum is 4.
+  - `timer`: the cumulative unwrapped device time in timer counts, or `null` when the CC had no pitch bend. The PB
+    value is `d1 | d2 << 7` (14-bit). Let `u = (value − prevValue) mod TIMER_MOD`, unsigned, because the timestamp
+    is a clock and never runs backwards. If `u ≥ TIMER_MARKER_MIN`, the delta is a **saturation marker**, and the
+    timer adds `u − TIMER_MOD` (−1 or −2) instead of `u`. Otherwise the timer adds `u`. So 16383 → 0 adds +1 and
+    0 → 16383 adds −1 (M4-03 step 2). A sample whose timer did not increase therefore means "≥ 5.8 ms since the
+    previous sample, untimed", and the estimator reads it that way. `timer` is always a plain counter that the
+    decoder never re-bases. The first pitch bend sets the reference and its sample gets `timer: 0`.
+  - `hostTimeUs`: the CC's host time.
+- **Pairing:** a CC is held as *pending*. It is emitted when its pitch bend arrives within `PAIR_WINDOW_US` (sample
+  with `timer`), or when the next CC arrives first (the pending one is emitted with `timer: null`, and the new one
+  becomes pending). A pitch bend with no pending CC, or arriving more than `PAIR_WINDOW_US` after it, is dropped and
+  counted (`stats().orphanPitchBends`, and the late CC is emitted with `timer: null`). Two CCs with no pitch bend
+  between them give two samples with `timer: null` (M4-03 step 2). Because pairing holds the CC back, a sample
+  normally comes out on the pitch bend's `feed`. `flush(hostTimeUs)` emits a pending CC older than
+  `PAIR_WINDOW_US` (as `timer: null`) and otherwise returns `null`. `pending()` returns `true` while a CC is held.
+  The glue and the tools call `flush` before each heartbeat (the estimator holds no decoder), so a lone last CC is
+  not lost, and they skip the heartbeat while `pending()` is still `true`. Otherwise a heartbeat could come between a
+  CC and its pitch bend and run ahead of that sample's time (75 capture pairs are 1 ms apart).
+- **The timer reference after a null-timer sample:** the next paired sample's `u` is still taken from the last
+  pitch bend seen. The device's counter runs on, so the delta covers the gap.
+- **A dropped pitch bend still moves the clock on** (decided while building M4-03): an orphan or late pitch bend
+  makes no sample, but its delta is added to the cumulative `timer` and its value becomes the reference, with the
+  same marker, error and untrusted rules as a paired one. CC 6 is a running counter, so the next sample's `ticks`
+  already covers a lost CC's ticks; adding the orphan's time keeps `timer` covering the same interval, and if the
+  timestamp really saturates per message (UNVERIFIED) skipping it would sum two deltas the device never sent as
+  one. The host gap for `TIMER_GAP_MAX_US` is therefore measured from the previous pitch bend, paired or not.
+  If a dropped pitch bend's delta was not timed (a marker, an unwrap error or untrusted), the next paired pitch
+  bend adds −1 instead of its `u`, so that sample's timer doesn't rise and it reads as untimed (M4-03 review).
+  For M4-05: after a late pitch bend, the null-timer sample's ticks fall inside the next timed sample's timer span,
+  so the estimator takes both its tick and its timer deltas from the last *timed* sample.
+- **Unwrap errors** (`stats().timerErrors`, which M4-03 step 3 requires to be 0 on the capture): a delta `u = 0`
+  between two pitch bends no more than `TIMER_GAP_MAX_US` apart, i.e. a clock that didn't advance while the host
+  saw continuous traffic (after a longer gap it counts as untrusted instead). The capture has none. Such a sample's timer adds the
+  −1 marker instead, so it is never used for timing.
+- **Untrusted timers** (`stats().untrustedTimers`, not an error): a non-marker `u` whose host gap since the
+  previous pitch bend (paired or not) exceeds `TIMER_GAP_MAX_US`. The timer adds the −1 marker instead of `u`. The decoder
+  still keeps the raw pitch-bend value as the reference for the next delta, so the cumulative `timer` is never
+  re-based. The capture has none (its largest such gap is 11 ms).
+- **Malformed input** (any byte not an integer in range, a status other than the two configured, a CC number other
+  than `config.cc`, a non-finite `hostTimeUs`): `feed` returns `null`, counts `stats().malformed` and leaves the
+  state untouched. A config that is not an object, has invalid values or throws on read falls back to `DEFAULTS`
+  key by key.
+- **Host clock steps** (D21, changed in M4-03): a `hostTimeUs` earlier than the last one is not malformed. The
+  glue's `Date.now()` is a wall clock and can step back (NTP, resume), and rejecting every message until it caught
+  up would leave the platter dead for as long as the step. The decoder adds the step to an offset, so its host
+  time carries on from the last value, and counts it in `stats().clockSteps`. Emitted `hostTimeUs` values are on
+  that absorbed clock (equal to the input until the first step).
+- **Lost CCs** (accepted, UNVERIFIED): a CC delta is read in [−64, 63], so more than 63 ticks lost in a row
+  (about 31 ms of messages at 33⅓, 21 ms in a −1.5× backspin) wraps into the wrong direction. The decoder can't see
+  it; M7 checks Mixxx's real delivery gaps.
+- **Stats:** `{ samples, orphanPitchBends, nullTimers, markers, timerErrors, untrustedTimers, malformed,
+  clockSteps }`.
+- **`untrusted: true`** (added in M4-05, D23): a sample whose timer got −1 for an untrusted delta or an unwrap
+  error, directly or through a dropped pitch bend's taint, carries this extra key. A marker's sample doesn't, so
+  the estimator can tell "no device time" from "at least 5.8 ms".
+
+## Speed estimator
+
+`createSpeedEstimator(config)` returns `{ push(sample), stats() }`, and `push` returns `{ ratio, timed }`.
+
+- **ratio** is the platter's speed relative to `NOMINAL_TICKS_PER_S` at `config.rpm`, signed: 1.0 is 33⅓ forward
+  (or 45 when `rpm: RPM_45`), and negative is backwards. **timed** is `true` when the ratio comes from device
+  timestamps and `false` when it comes from host time.
+- **Timed path.** For a sample with a valid timer delta (`dTimer > 0`, not a marker), the instantaneous ratio is
+  `dTicks · TIMER_PER_TICK / dTimer`. The timestamp is that of the message's last tick (plan one-liner: in 20–36 s,
+  1-tick messages have a median delta of 1413, 2-tick 2823, 3-tick 4234, 4-tick 5661), so multi-tick messages need
+  no special case. The filter (below) runs in device time (`dTimer / TIMER_HZ` seconds). Host batching does not
+  touch it.
+- **Untimed path.** The ratio is `(ticks − ticks at hostTimeUs − HOST_WINDOW_MS) / window seconds /
+  NOMINAL_TICKS_PER_S`. The estimator keeps a short ring of `(hostTimeUs, ticks)`, and the window shrinks to the
+  data it has (as built: the position at the window's start is interpolated between the two pushes around it).
+  ~~It is also used when the filtered |ratio| falls below `MIN_TIMED_SPEED_RATIO`, resuming at 1.25 × that.~~
+  **Changed in M4-05 (D23):** there is no speed gate. A valid timer delta is timed however slow, because below
+  0.086 every delta is a marker anyway, and with the gate every scratch reversal fell into the 200 ms window,
+  which read about 0.9 at the turn. As built:
+  - A valid delta (`dTimer > 0`) is timed. After any untimed sample, it re-seeds the filter.
+  - A saturation marker, whatever the host gap, takes the host-time ratio clamped between 0 and the smaller of
+    `dTicks · TIMER_PER_TICK / TIMER_MARKER_MIN` (0.086 per tick) and `dTicks` over the host time since `ref`, in
+    the ticks' direction: a marker means at least 5.8 ms since the device's last tick.
+  - A sample the decoder flags `untrusted: true` (a host gap over `TIMER_GAP_MAX_US`, or an unwrap error) has no
+    device time. Within `STALL_MS` of the last timed sample it is most likely the first of a host-side burst (its
+    ticks are older than its host time) and keeps the timed ratio, as heartbeats do. Otherwise it takes the host
+    path unclamped.
+  - A `null` timer (lost pitch bend) within `TIMER_GAP_MAX_US` of the last timed sample keeps the timed ratio
+    (the next delta from `ref` covers its ticks); later it takes the host path.
+  - The ring is trimmed to the window on every push (`stats().ringSize`), so a long timed run can't grow it.
+- **Heartbeat.** A still platter sends nothing, so without new samples the ratio would stay at its last value.
+  The glue (M7, from an `engine.beginTimer` every 20 ms) and the tools push a heartbeat
+  `{ ticks: <last ticks>, timer: null, hostTimeUs: <now> }`. It goes through the untimed path and decays the ratio
+  to 0 within `HOST_WINDOW_MS`. As built (M4-05 review): after `STALL_MS` a heartbeat's ratio is also clamped to
+  one tick in the time since the last real sample, because none came; otherwise a back-cue ending in a dead stop
+  read the window's −0.99 and T1 would fire. **For M7:** heartbeats must be timed on the decoder's absorbed clock
+  (D21), not raw `Date.now()`. After a backward step of the wall clock, raw heartbeats are clamped to the last
+  sample's time and the ratio holds for as long as the step (review finding, not fixed in M4-05).
+- **Stalls vs a stopped hand.** A heartbeat changes the ratio only after `STALL_MS` (default 50, **UNVERIFIED**)
+  with no real sample. Before that it returns the previous result. Without this, a 20–40 ms gap in Mixxx's MIDI
+  delivery at motor speed would read as about 0.9 over the 200 ms window, and T7 would take control mid-track. A
+  real hand on a motor-driven record doesn't go silent: the capture's hold phase keeps sending about 16 marker
+  samples per second (38–41 s). Those go through the untimed path at once, so a hand stop is still seen within one
+  sample. A host stall longer than `STALL_MS` drops the ratio to about 0 at the first heartbeat after it (as built
+  in M4-05: one tick in the silence, so 0.008 at 60 ms), so T7 takes over as `held`, which lasts until the burst's
+  timed samples arrive (the burst's first, `untrusted` sample reads the window, 0.66 after 70 ms and 0.41 after
+  120 ms). That is a known limitation: the burst that follows then drives `position` under platter control by the
+  ticks the platter really turned. M4-06 should expect a stall-made `held`.
+- **Repeats and time order.** A sample identical to the previous one (same `ticks`, `timer` and `hostTimeUs`) is
+  a no-op that returns the previous result (`stats().repeats`). A real sample whose `hostTimeUs` is earlier than
+  the last push is not dropped: its host time is clamped to the last one (`stats().clampedTimes`), because its
+  ticks are real. Only a non-finite time, or a non-integer `ticks`, is malformed.
+- **Candidate filters** (M4-05 measures both and picks one, recorded as `D22 *default*`; its step 2 says
+  `D13 *default*`, but D13 is taken, and D21 went to M4-03's host-clock rule):
+  - **α-β** on position against device time: predict `x̂ += v̂·Δt`, residual `r = x − x̂`, `x̂ += α·r`,
+    `v̂ += (β/Δt)·r`, and `ratio = v̂ / NOMINAL_TICKS_PER_S`. Two parameters (α, β). It is what Mixxx's own
+    `scratch2` filter is, so reviewers know it.
+  - **1-Euro** (Casiez et al. 2012) on the instantaneous ratio: a low-pass whose cutoff rises with speed of change,
+    `fc = minCutoff + beta·|dx̂|`, with derivative cutoff `dCutoff`. Three parameters. It filters hard at steady
+    speed and lightly during a scratch.
+- **Synthetic data used to pick** (all from `tools/synth-platter`, M4-04, fixed seeds): `constant33` (ratio 1.0,
+  `jitterTicks: 1`), `constant45` (with `rpm: RPM_45`), `step` (1.0 for 2 s, then 0.5 for 2 s), `scratch`
+  (sinusoid, 200 ticks amplitude, 0.5 s period), `backspin`, `slow` (0.02), `drop-and-burst` (10 % drop, a 40 ms
+  host-side stall then a burst). The constant segments use the capture-fitted timer jitter (`timerJitterCounts`
+  4.5, see synth-platter), so the filters are tuned against noise as large as the One's, not exact timestamps.
+  That noise is white on each timestamp, though. The One's is mostly slow wow (see synth-platter, As built), so
+  the synth's fast noise is about 4.8 times the real fast part, and the synth has no wow. M4-05 should report its
+  real-capture 50 ms jitter beside the synthetic one. Each
+  filter's parameters are tuned by a small grid search on `constant33` + `step` only. The other scripts then test
+  them unseen.
+- **Measured per filter:** steady-state peak |ratio − 1| after 50 ms (constant33, constant45); settle time to
+  0.5 ± 0.02 after the step; scratch lag (time shift of best fit, ms) and peak error; max |Δratio| between
+  `drop-and-burst` and its undropped twin.
+- **Acceptance** (both filters must reach it, and failing it rules a filter out): 33⅓ with ±1 tick jitter gives
+  ratio **1.000 ± 0.002 for every sample after the first 50 ms**; the same on constant45 with `RPM_45`; a step to
+  0.5 is within **0.5 ± 0.02 within 100 ms**; dropped messages change the ratio by **< 0.01**; on `slow`, `timed`
+  is false and the ratio is within **±20 %** of 0.02; on `backspin` it goes negative. Separately, on the
+  `drop-and-burst` stall (40 ms, under `STALL_MS`), the ratio changes by **< 0.01** and returns no ratio below
+  `1 − TAKEOVER_TOL`. **Pick rule:** the smaller
+  scratch lag, and on a tie (within 1 ms) α-β, for fewer parameters and familiarity upstream.
+- **Seeding:** the first valid timed sample seeds the filter with its instantaneous ratio (α-β: `v̂` from it,
+  `x̂ = x`). A cold start therefore doesn't sit at 0 for the first 50 ms.
+- **Stats:** `{ pushed, timed, untimed, reseeds, repeats, clampedTimes, heldOverStall, malformed }`, plus `ringSize`. As built,
+  `heldOverStall` counts every push that returned the previous result for want of device time: a heartbeat within
+  `STALL_MS`, a burst's first sample and a lost pitch bend (D23).
+
+### Filter choice (M4-05, D22)
+
+Measured with `node tests/helpers/filter-bench.mjs`, which generates the fixtures with their seeds, runs decoder →
+estimator through `tests/helpers/run-core.mjs` (a heartbeat every 20 ms of `t_us`, as `tools/platter-run` will) and
+grid-searches each filter on `constant33` + `step` only. The objective is the fastest settle to 0.5 ± 0.01 after the
+step, among configs whose `constant33` peak |r − 1| stays within 0.001, half the ±0.002 acceptance. The grid was
+α ∈ 0.05–0.8 × β ∈ 0.005–0.5 for α-β (72 stable configs, 43 within the peak), and min cutoff 1–50 Hz × beta 0–100
+× derivative cutoff 1–20 Hz for 1-Euro (90 configs, 68 within the peak). All other columns are unseen data.
+
+| Measure | α-β (α 0.5, β 0.1) | **1-Euro (1 Hz, β 10, 20 Hz)** |
+|---|---|---|
+| constant33, peak \|r − 1\| after 50 ms (sd) | 0.00074 (0.00014) | **0.00038 (0.00007)** |
+| constant33, 50 ms means | 0.99991–1.00009 | 0.99984–1.00014 |
+| constant45 with `RPM_45`, peak | 0.00060 | **0.00039** |
+| step 1.0 → 0.5, settle to ±0.02 / ±0.01 | 13 / 14 ms | **3 / 3 ms** |
+| scratch (200 ticks, 0.5 s), lag of best fit | 5.5 ms | **2 ms** |
+| scratch, peak error at that lag / at none | 0.112 / 0.198 | **0.063 / 0.095** |
+| scratch, readings below −0.5 while truly above −0.4 | 0 | 0 |
+| drop-and-burst vs its undropped twin, max \|Δr\| | 0.00088 | **0.00028** |
+| **real capture 20–36 s**, peak \|r − 1\| (sd) | 0.0047 (0.0015) | **0.0036 (0.0013)** |
+| real capture 20–36 s, 50 ms means | 0.9970–1.0027 | 0.9971–1.0026 |
+
+- **Pick (the rule above): 1-Euro.** Its scratch lag is 3.5 ms shorter, beyond the 1 ms tie. It is ahead on every
+  other line too. Both pass every acceptance test in `tests/platter-speed.test.mjs`, which runs on both, and that
+  test also checks that `DEFAULTS.FILTER` is still the rule's pick.
+- **Synthetic vs real jitter.** On synthetic data the 50 ms means stay within ±0.00016. On the real motor phase
+  they span 0.997–1.0027, the same for both filters. That spread is the motor's slow wow (synth-platter, As built),
+  which any filter that settles in milliseconds has to pass, and it sits well inside M4-07's ±0.01. The real fast
+  noise is smaller than the synthetic, so tuning on the synth is on the safe side.
+- **Grid edges.** 1-Euro's settle is at its 3 ms floor (the step's first few messages), so a wider grid can't
+  settle faster. Its min cutoff sits at the grid's low edge (1 Hz), but the peak barely moves with it (1 → 5 Hz:
+  0.00038 → 0.00044). α-β's pick is inside its grid.
+- **Before D23**, with the plan's speed gate, the first bench run gave a scratch peak error of 1.02 (1-Euro at 10 Hz,
+  β 1, 5 Hz) and 1.22 (α-β at 0.1, 0.005), from 15–16 untimed samples at the reversals.
+- **What this doesn't show:** the parameters are UNVERIFIED on deck 2, at 45 rpm on hardware, at torque LOW and in
+  Mixxx's 5 ms poll batching (M5, M7). With 1 Hz / β 10 / 20 Hz the 1-Euro follows the timestamps almost raw. A
+  single outlier timestamp passes through for one message. `TAKEOVER_MS` (10 ms) keeps that from flipping
+  control in `motor_at_speed` (T7), but T14 acts at once in `settling`, so one outlier can restart settling.
+  The capture's 20–36 s has none beyond ±0.0036.
+
+## State machine
+
+`createStateMachine(config)` returns `{ update(input), stats() }`, with
+`update({ ratio, hostTimeUs, expectedRatio, ticks, playRatio })` returning `{ state, control, position }`.
+
+M4-02 step 3 fixes `update({ratio, hostTimeUs, expectedRatio})` returning `{state, control}`. The plan adds
+`ticks` (the decoder's cumulative ticks) to the input and `position` to the result, because M4-06 step 3 tests a
+returned `position` against the integrated platter position. Integrating `ratio` over host time instead would
+drift from the real ticks. It also adds an optional `playRatio`: the rate Mixxx's clock plays at (the pitch
+fader's `rate_ratio`, 0 while the deck is paused), which defaults to `expectedRatio`. The two differ if the One's
+motor ignores the pitch fader.
+
+- **state** is one of `stopped`, `motor_at_speed`, `held`, `scratching`, `backspin`, `settling`. **control** is
+  `clock` in `stopped` and `motor_at_speed`, and `platter` in the other four (M4-06 step 2).
+- **expectedRatio** `e` is the speed the platter should turn at untouched, relative to `config.rpm`. The glue
+  sets it: 0 while the motor is off (or the deck isn't expected to turn), otherwise 1.0, or the pitch fader's
+  `rate_ratio` if the One's motor follows the fader. Which of those holds is **UNVERIFIED** (spec §6 step 5). In
+  the capture the motor toggles with note 0 ch 1 (Play is the guess), so `tools/platter-run` derives `e` from
+  those presses (Real-capture check).
+- **Notation:** `r` = ratio, `dev = r/e − 1` when `e > 0`. "For T ms" means the condition held on every update for
+  the last T ms of `hostTimeUs`. Each condition has its own timer, which starts when the condition first holds and
+  resets when it breaks. The initial state is `stopped`. "Since `e` rose" counts from the last update where `e`
+  went from 0 to > 0. If `e > 0` on the very first update, that update counts as the rise.
+- **Transitions,** checked in this order on each update; the first that matches wins, otherwise the state stays:
+
+| # | From | To | Condition |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| T1 | motor_at_speed, held, scratching, settling | backspin | `e > 0` and `r < BACKSPIN_RATIO` (−0.5), immediately. With the motor off, a fast backward turn is a hand turn and stays `scratching`: the capture's backward hand turn reaches −0.6 at 9.4–9.8 s (358 per-message ratios below −0.5, plan one-liner) |
+| T2 | backspin | stopped | `e = 0` and \|r\| ≤ `STOPPED_RATIO` for `STOP_MS` |
+| T3 | backspin | scratching | `e > 0` and `r ≥ BACKSPIN_EXIT_RATIO` (−0.4): the motor pulls the record forward again. With `e = 0` a backspin stays `backspin` until T2, so the decay ends in `stopped` |
+| T4 | stopped | motor_at_speed | `e > 0` and \|dev\| ≤ `HANDBACK_TOL` for `SETTLE_MS` |
+| T5 | stopped | scratching | `e = 0` and \|r\| > `MOVE_RATIO` for `TAKEOVER_MS` |
+| T5a | stopped | held | `e > 0` and \|r\| ≤ `HELD_RATIO` for `HELD_START_MS` (150) since `e` rose: a record held through Play. Plan one-liner: a free spin-up passes 0.10 within 50 ms of the 19.236 s press (25 ms windows: 0.00, 0.10, 0.26, 0.38, …), so 150 ms is 3× that (**UNVERIFIED** at torque LOW) |
+| T6 | stopped | held / scratching | `e > 0`, more than `SPINUP_MS` since `e` rose, and \|dev\| > `HANDBACK_TOL`: held if \|r\| ≤ `HELD_RATIO`, else scratching |
+| T7 | motor_at_speed | held / scratching | \|dev\| > `TAKEOVER_TOL` for `TAKEOVER_MS`: held if \|r\| ≤ `HELD_RATIO`, else scratching |
+| T8 | motor_at_speed, settling | scratching | `e` becomes 0 (motor stopped: the spin-down runs under platter control, like a turntable; **UNVERIFIED** choice, M7 checks it in Mixxx) |
+| T9 | held | scratching | \|r\| > `HELD_EXIT_RATIO` (0.2) |
+| T10 | scratching | held | `e > 0` and \|r\| ≤ `HELD_RATIO` for `TAKEOVER_MS` |
+| T11 | scratching, held | settling | `e > 0` and \|dev\| ≤ `HANDBACK_TOL` |
+| T12 | scratching, held | stopped | `e = 0` and \|r\| ≤ `STOPPED_RATIO` for `STOP_MS` |
+| T13 | settling | motor_at_speed | \|dev\| ≤ `HANDBACK_TOL` for `SETTLE_MS` (the timer starts on entering settling and restarts whenever \|dev\| > `HANDBACK_TOL`) |
+| T14 | settling | held / scratching | \|dev\| > `TAKEOVER_TOL`, immediately (control is already `platter`, so nothing flips): held if \|r\| ≤ `HELD_RATIO`, else scratching |
+
+- **Hysteresis:** control leaves the clock only beyond ±3 % (T7) and returns only within ±1 % held for
+  `SETTLE_MS` (T11, T13). A ratio oscillating at ±2 % therefore never flips control. In `motor_at_speed` it stays
+  under 3 %, and in `scratching`/`settling` it never gets within 1 % long enough (M4-06 step 2).
+- **Which jitter M4-06 step 1 means:** the synth `constant33` stream (message-boundary ±1 tick plus the fitted
+  timer jitter) through the estimator. That moves the ratio by under 0.5 % per message (the real capture:
+  0.9945–1.0041), so `motor_at_speed` stays put. Position dither (±1 tick back and forth, as in the hold phase)
+  would give ±50 % per message, but it arrives as markers through the untimed path. M4-06 tests it on `held`,
+  not on `motor_at_speed`.
+- **An `expectedRatio` change** while in `motor_at_speed` re-bases `dev` at once. If the platter follows (the
+  ratio tracks the new `e` within `TAKEOVER_MS`), nothing transitions (M4-06 step 3). If the motor turns out to
+  follow the fader with lag, `RETARGET_MS > 0` widens the at-speed band to cover both the old and the new `e`
+  for that long. It stays off until M5 measures the lag.
+- **Malformed input** (a non-finite `ratio`, `ticks` or `expectedRatio`, a negative `expectedRatio`, a
+  `hostTimeUs` that goes backwards, a non-object): `update` returns the previous result unchanged and counts
+  `stats().malformed` (M4-06 step 4).
+- **Stats:** `{ updates, transitions: { "<from>><to>": n }, malformed }`.
+
+## Switch-over
+
+The state machine decides who drives the track. `position` makes sure the handover never jumps.
+
+- **`position`** is the track position in platter ticks (float). Mixxx's frame is 1 tick = 60 / (rpm ·
+  TICKS_PER_REV) s of audio at ratio 1, which is 0.5 ms (24 frames at 48 kHz) at 33⅓. M7 converts.
+  - Under `clock` it is the clock's model: `position += playRatio · NOMINAL_TICKS_PER_S · Δt` per update (M7
+    replaces this model with Mixxx's `playposition`; telemetry D11 compares the two).
+  - Under `platter` it is the **integrated platter position**:
+    `position = positionAtTakeover + (ticks − ticksAtTakeover)`.
+- **The switch is defined by `control`, not by transition number.** Takeover is any update where `control` goes
+  `clock` → `platter` (today T1, T5, T5a, T6, T7, T8). Handback is any update where it goes `platter` → `clock`
+  (today T2, T12, T13). T4 is clock → clock and switches nothing. The implementation compares `control` before and
+  after each update, so a transition added later can't skip the snapshot.
+- **Takeover:** `positionAtTakeover` = the clock position at that update, and `ticksAtTakeover` = that update's
+  `ticks`. The first platter-mode position equals the last clock position, so there is no jump.
+- **Handback invariant:** **on the update that hands back, the returned `position`
+  equals the integrated platter position `positionAtTakeover + (ticks − ticksAtTakeover)` (minus the ticks D24
+  absorbed after a confirmed host stall, 0 otherwise) to within one tick,
+  and the clock resumes from exactly that value.** The clock never snaps back to where it would have been had
+  nobody touched the record. So the music continues from where the hand left it, with no jump (M4-06 step 3).
+- **Speed at handback:** T13 only fires after `SETTLE_MS` within ±1 % of `e`. If `playRatio = e`, the speed step
+  at the switch is therefore under 1 %, spread by Mixxx's own rate ramp in M7. If the motor ignores the pitch fader
+  (`e` 1.0, `playRatio` the fader's rate), the speed steps by \|playRatio − e\| at every handback, however clean
+  the position is. M5 (§6 step 5) has to settle that before M7 picks a design. T2 and T12 hand back with the
+  platter still (`e = 0`, Mixxx paused), so there is no speed step.
+- **As built (M4-06):**
+  - The condition timers count from no earlier than entering the state, which is the T13 rule made general.
+    Given the conditions, only T13 can tell the difference: on held → T9 → T11 the band holds one update before
+    settling.
+  - T5a needs `e > 0` in its condition, so its run can't start before `e` rose. "Since `e` rose" therefore needs
+    no timer of its own.
+  - Under the clock, each update adds the previous update's `playRatio` over the interval.
+  - Malformed input isn't counted in `updates`.
+  - `RETARGET_MS` keeps the old `e` as a second at-speed reference for T7 only.
+  - **D24 *default*** (a deviation from the takeover rule above): a takeover from `motor_at_speed` with no tick
+    change for `STALL_MS` is armed. If the first tick change after it is the decoder's `untrusted` sample, that
+    confirms a host stall, and the next forward ticks (up to the clock's advance since the last tick) are
+    absorbed, so the burst doesn't move the track twice. Measured: up to 81 ms of skip per stall without it.
+    Absorbing ends `PAIR_WINDOW_US` after the confirming sample (only the burst). D24's dead-stop safety rests
+    on M5 check 2's saturation reading. Under the wrap hypothesis a release would read `untrusted`.
+    A dead hand stop releases with a marker and absorbs nothing. `update` gains an optional `untrusted` input
+    (the glue passes the decoder sample's flag), and `tests/helpers/run-core.mjs` reports it. Where D24 absorbs,
+    the handback invariant reads: position = `positionAtTakeover + (ticks − ticksAtTakeover) − absorbed`.
+  - `playRatio` `null` is malformed, and a negative one runs the clock backwards. A forward jump in
+    `hostTimeUs` is not guarded: one outlier 60 s ahead moves the clock 120,000 ticks, and every later update is
+    then rejected as backwards (review Minor). **M7 must feed the decoder's monotonic clock**, and if that can't
+    be guaranteed, decide on a resync rule there.
+  - Known for M7: in `stopped` with `e > 0` the clock plays at `e` during the spin-up. A record held through
+    Play (T5a) has therefore already played `HELD_START_MS` of audio (300 ticks) before the platter takes over.
+- **What M7 must still decide (not M4):** which Mixxx control carries `position` under platter control. The spec
+  names `scratch_position`, so M7 checks that 2.5.6 has it, or picks the best available control and records why.
+  M7 also decides how the glue reads Mixxx's position at takeover. The core only promises that `position` is
+  continuous across every control change.
+
+## synth-platter
+
+`tools/synth-platter --script <motion.json> --out <file.jsonl> [--seed N]` (M4-04). It writes capture-format
+JSONL, the same lines `tools/capture-convert` writes (`t_us`, `bytes`, `type`, `ch`, `d1`, `d2`, `value`), which
+`tools/analyze-platter`, the decoder tests and `tools/replay` read unchanged.
+
+- **Device model** (from the capture, D19): an ideal tick clock from the motion script. Messages go out at most once
+  per `messagePeriodUs` (default 1000, the 998–999/s cap). Each carries every tick since the last message
+  (CC 6 = position mod 128) and the timestamp of its **last** tick (PB = device time in counts mod 16384,
+  at `TIMER_HZ`). If more than 16,383 counts passed since the previous message, PB = previous − 1 (the saturation
+  marker, `saturate: true`, the default). `saturate: false` models the Twelve hypothesis instead, where the counter
+  simply wraps, so the decoder's wrap path has data. The CC and PB share one `t_us`, rounded to 1 ms like
+  `arecordmidi`.
+- **Motion script:** `{ "rpm": 33.333, "seed": 1, "segments": [ … ] }`. The segments are:
+  - `{kind: "constant", ratio, seconds, jitterTicks}`. Each message's send time is shifted by a seeded uniform
+    ±`jitterTicks` · tick period / 2, so the ticks per message vary by ±`jitterTicks`, as in the capture
+    (+1 ×127, +2 ×15,679, +3 ×165 at 1.0). The timestamp stays that of the last tick actually folded in, rounded to
+    whole counts, plus seeded Gaussian timer noise of `timerJitterCounts` (default 4.5 counts: the standard
+    deviation of the capture's per-message ratio in 20–36 s is 0.158 %, which is 4.5 counts on a 2-tick delta;
+    real 2-tick deltas run 2811–2838). This is message-boundary jitter, not position noise. The hold-phase ±1
+    dither (position noise) is what M4-06 tests separately on `held`.
+  - `scratch`: sinusoidal back-and-forth, `{amplitudeTicks, periodSeconds, seconds}`.
+  - `backspin`: `{fromRatio (negative), decaySeconds, seconds}`, an exponential decay to 0.
+  - `slow`: `{ratio: 0.02, seconds}`. Every message is then a marker under `saturate: true`. M4-04 step 2's "deltas
+    that wrap (|delta| > 8192)" holds for unsigned deltas (16383) in both models.
+  - `drop`: `{percent, seconds, ratio}`, removing that share of CC+PB pairs after generation (host-side loss,
+    seeded). The next message still carries the true position, so the net ticks are unchanged.
+  - `stall-burst`: `{stallMs, ratio, seconds}`, a **host-side** stall. The device keeps sending (its timestamps
+    and ticks are unchanged), but for `stallMs` nothing reaches the host. Then the held-back messages all arrive
+    with the burst's `t_us`. A device-side stall would be a `constant` segment at ratio 0.
+- **Fixtures:** `tests/fixtures/motion/{constant33,constant45,scratch,backspin,slow,drop-and-burst,step}.json`,
+  committed. Their JSONL is generated into a temp dir by the tests' setup, not committed (M4-04 step 3).
+- **Determinism:** a seeded PRNG (mulberry32), so the same script and seed give byte-identical output. Each
+  generated file's expected net ticks is exactly `round(∫ ratio · NOMINAL_TICKS_PER_S dt)` over the segments,
+  and M4-04 step 4 checks the decoder against it.
+- **As built (M4-04),** where the build pins down what the plan left open. The code is
+  `tools/lib/synth-platter.mjs`, and `tests/synth-platter.test.mjs` tests each point:
+  - **Ticks:** the record starts half a tick in (x = 0.5), so n = floor(x) ends at round(∫). The first poll (t = 0)
+    always sends, and so does a last poll at the script's end, so the decoder's net ticks equal the scripted ones
+    exactly. Its reference timestamp is the tick half a tick before t = 0 at the starting speed, so the first
+    delta is whole ticks too. A poll whose n is unchanged sends nothing (the capture has 0 CC deltas of 0).
+  - **Poll phase:** the polls after the first run from a seeded phase. With the polls on whole ms and x = 0.5,
+    every tick at 1.0 sits exactly half a tick period from a poll, the one alignment where a ± half-period shift
+    never moves a tick. The share of messages that jitter moves depends on that phase: `constant33` has 568 of
+    8000 (7 %) in 1–9 s, against the capture's 292 of 15,971 (1.8 %).
+  - **After a marker** the next delta counts from the marker message's real time, not from absolute device time.
+    The pitch bend works as an accumulator: it adds each delta, or adds −1 at saturation. Capture: all 4
+    marker → real transitions read 9740–15075 counts (3.5–5.3 ms) after 4–5 ms of host time (2.750, 9.160, 19.275,
+    41.317 s). An absolute clock would put them anywhere in 0–16383. The 7 markers of −2 aren't modelled.
+  - **Timer noise** goes on each timestamp with sd `timerJitterCounts / √2`, so a delta has sd 4.5 and the timer
+    doesn't random-walk (5 s total within 30 counts, tested). **For M4-05:** in the capture most of that 4.5 is
+    slow motor wow, not per-message noise. In 20–36 s the 50-message means of a 2-tick delta range 2814–2831
+    (sd 3.6), the sd of successive differences is only 1.63, and the lag-1 autocorrelation is +0.93. The synth's
+    white noise is an upper bound on the fast part and doesn't model the wow. That wow is why the real capture
+    gets 1.00 ± 0.01 and not ± 0.002.
+  - **"|delta| > 8192" (M4-04 step 2)** holds as the unsigned marker 16383 (the default `saturate: true`). A
+    signed 14-bit delta never exceeds 8192. With `saturate: false` at 0.02, a 25 ms tick is 70,560 counts, which
+    reads as 5024 every time: the counter wraps 4 times per tick, and nothing in the stream shows it.
+  - **Host time** is the nominal poll time rounded to the ms: the send jitter picks which ticks a message carries,
+    but the host sees messages a whole period apart. Interior polls stay a whole period from the first and the
+    last, so no two messages share a ms outside a stall burst, for any seed (tested). The final message can span up
+    to two periods. The capture's host gaps also vary by ±1 ms in 5.4 % of 20–36 s. That isn't modelled.
+  - **Limits:** |ratio| ≤ 10, and the peak ticks per message (from rpm, ticksPerRev, ratio or the scratch's peak,
+    and `messagePeriodUs`) must be ≤ 48, so CC 6 can't alias. A drop that would leave 64 or more ticks between two
+    messages the host gets is refused, and nothing is written. `stall-burst`'s stall sits in the middle of its segment unless `atSeconds` says otherwise. The burst
+    arrives at the stall's end, rounded to the ms. `drop` removes exactly round(percent · messages) of its
+    segment's messages, never the file's first or last. A twin with `percent: 0` and the same seed differs only
+    by the removed messages.
+  - **For M4-05:** `drop-and-burst`'s 40 ms stall is under `STALL_MS` but over the decoder's `TIMER_GAP_MAX_US`
+    (20 ms), so the burst's first sample is untimed (`untrustedTimers` 1, tested). The stall acceptance has to
+    allow that sample.
+
+## Real-capture check
+
+What the core must give on `captures/2026-10-19-quick/platter.jsonl` (M4-03 and M4-07), with the numbers measured
+while writing this plan:
+
+- **Decoder** (M4-03 step 3): 29,323 CC 6 and 29,323 pitch bends, so **29,323 samples**, all paired, 0 orphan
+  pitch bends. The net ticks equal the sum of the signed CC deltas, **+43,547** (plan one-liner). There are
+  **281 markers** (274 at −1, 7 at −2), **0 zero deltas** (`timerErrors` 0) and a largest non-marker delta of
+  16,304.
+- **Estimator, motor phase 20–36 s:** the timed per-message ratio (`dTicks · 1411.2 / dTimer`) runs from 0.9945 to
+  1.0041, median 0.9998 (p0.5–p99.5: 0.9956–1.0030), and timed 50 ms windows stay within ±0.31 % (plan
+  one-liners). The real motor is not exactly 1.000 over 50 ms (the D19 caveat: 3598 vs 3600), so the real-capture
+  tolerance is M4-07's 1.00 ± 0.01. The ±0.002 acceptance applies to the synthetic `constant33` only.
+- **`tools/platter-run`** (M4-07) runs decoder → estimator → state machine over the file, heartbeating every
+  20 ms of `t_us`. It derives `expectedRatio` from note 0 ch 1 presses: off at the start, 1.0 after the first
+  press, 0 after the next. This assumes the button is Play (**UNVERIFIED**, D19). `--expected <t>:<e>,…` overrides
+  it. Expected timeline (from the capture's host-time ratios over 50–200 ms windows):
+  - 3–7 s `scratching`, ratio +0.26 to +0.53 (p5–p95 per message, median 0.42; motor off, hand forward), and
+    10–14 s `scratching`, ratio −0.28 to −0.43 (p5–p95, minimum −0.45). Earlier, 9.4–9.8 s reaches −0.6, but with
+    `e = 0` T1 doesn't apply, so it stays `scratching` and not `backspin`;
+  - 19.236 s press: `stopped` through the spin-up (at speed ≈ 19.6 s), then `motor_at_speed` by ≈ 19.75 s
+    (T4 after `SETTLE_MS`), comfortably before M4-07's 21 s, holding to 36 s with no control flip;
+  - ≈ 37.7 s the hand lands (50 ms ratio 0.39, then 0.00): T7 → `scratching` (r ≈ 0.85 at that point), then T10
+    → `held` by ≈ 37.9 s (Opus review estimate), staying `held` (|r| ≤ 0.0225) through 38.5–40.5 s;
+  - ≈ 41.35 s release: back to about 1.0 by 41.4 s, but 50 ms windows overshoot to 1.015, and |dev| > 1 % lasts
+    until ≈ 41.86 s. So `settling`, then `motor_at_speed` by ≈ 42.0 s (M4-07: by 42.5 s, a 0.5 s margin);
+  - 44.670 s press: T8 → `scratching` through the spin-down (|r| 0.72 → 0.045 by 45.3 s, ≤ 0.02 from 45.6 s),
+    then `stopped` by ≈ 45.9 s (M4-07: after 46 s). The margin is narrow. The 200 ms wander from 46.0 to 46.3 s
+    is 0.018, against a `STOPPED_RATIO` of 0.03.
+- **Glitches:** M4-07 lists every control flip lasting under 20 ms. With `TAKEOVER_MS` 10 and the hysteresis above,
+  the plan expects none on this capture. If there are any, they are reported with their times, not tuned away
+  silently.
+
+- **As built (M4-07):** `tools/platter-run` (loop in `tools/lib/platter-run.mjs`, which `tests/helpers/run-core.mjs`
+  now wraps) gives the timeline above with 0 glitches. The times it measured: `motor_at_speed` 19.642 s, `held`
+  37.791 s, `settling` 41.371 s, `motor_at_speed` 42.004 s, `stopped` 45.671 s. The timeline and the margins are
+  in the analysis document under "What the core sees". `--expected` takes `<seconds>:<ratio>` pairs in rising
+  time. A press is a note 0 ch 1 note-on with velocity > 0, and `expectedRatio` changes at the press, for the messages
+  after it in the file (at 44.670 s the CC 6 and pitch bend of that millisecond come before the note-on, so their
+  sample still has `expectedRatio` 1). A heartbeat at exactly a change's time comes before it, with `--expected`
+  as with presses. The timeline keeps that order.
+
+## Order of work
+
+M4-03 decoder (tests first) → M4-04 synth-platter → M4-05 estimator and the filter choice (D22) → M4-06 state
+machine and switch-over → M4-07 platter-run on the real capture → M4-08 review.
