+# The quick capture, analysed (M4-01)
+
+- **Capture:** `captures/2026-10-19-quick/platter.jsonl` (58,650 messages over 48.39 s; deck 1: slow turn forward
+  and back by hand, motor on 15 s, hold 3 s, stop). It is `platter.mid` converted by `tools/capture-convert`.
+  `arecordmidi` wrote it with 1 ms resolution, so `t_us` is host time rounded to the millisecond.
+- **Tool:** `tools/analyze-platter` (M4-01). Every number below is printed by
+  `tools/analyze-platter captures/2026-10-19-quick/platter.jsonl --phases 2-8,9-15,20-36,38-41,41-44,45-49`
+  unless the line names another command. The output is pasted at the end.
+- **Conventions:** a phase is `[from, to)` in seconds of `t_us`, clipped to the capture's first and last message
+  (2.651 s and 48.39 s), so rates are per second of data. A delta is the change from the previous message of the
+  same kind: CC 6 deltas are taken mod 128 and timestamp deltas mod 16384, both signed, in [−m/2, m/2), for the
+  histograms. The net timer is the sum of the **unsigned** deltas (the timestamp is a clock) without the −1s, which
+  are counted apart ("at −1"). "Ticks per revolution" means net ticks / (seconds · 33⅓ / 60), i.e. it assumes the
+  motor turned at exactly 33⅓.
+- **Status:** these are facts about one capture of one deck on one unit. Anything this capture can't show is
+  marked UNVERIFIED. Where it contradicts `2026-10-19-prior-art.md` §2, the capture wins (decision D19).
+
+## What the capture shows
+
+| Phase (s) | What Michael did | CC 6 msgs/s (whole seconds) | CC 6 delta per msg | timestamp delta per msg | net ticks | ticks/rev at 33⅓ |
+|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
+| 2–8 (data from 2.651) | slow turn forward by hand | 664.2 (402–908) | +1 ×3536, +2 ×16 | scattered, 2031 distinct values | +3568 | (hand) |
+| 9–15 | slow turn back by hand | 607.8 (217–762) | −1 ×3610, −2 ×33, +1 ×4 | scattered, −1 ×53 | −3672 | (hand) |
+| 20–36 | motor on, untouched | **998.3 (998–999)** | **+2 ×15679** of 15972 (+1 ×127, +3 ×165, +4 ×1) | **2819–2826 for 9665 of 15972**, net 45,159,496 | **+31,984** | **3598.2** |
+| 38–41 | record held still, motor on | 16.7 (16–18) | −1 ×24, +1 ×26 | **−1 ×49**, −2 ×1 | +2 | — |
+| 41–44 | released, motor | 892.3 (680–999) | +2 ×2590, +1 ×47, +3 ×39, −1 ×1 | 2818–2825 most often | +5343 | 3205.8 (includes the spin-up) |
+| 45–49 (data to 48.39) | motor stopped | 25.1 (5–59) | +1 ×59, −1 ×26 | −1 ×78, −2 ×2, five spin-down values | +33 | — |
+
+### The motor phase (20–36 s)
+
+- **Rate:** 15,972 CC 6 messages in 16 s, 998.3/s; every whole second from 20 to 35 holds 998 or 999. Each CC 6
+  is followed by one pitch bend, so the stream is about 2,000 messages/s, capped at about one pair per millisecond.
+- **CC 6 advances 2 per message:** 15,679 of 15,972 deltas are +2 (127 are +1, 165 are +3, 1 is +4). The device
+  sends at most about one message per millisecond and folds the ticks in between into it, so a decoder must accept
+  multi-tick messages.
+- **The timestamp advances about 2822 per message:** the eight most frequent deltas are 2819–2826 (9,665 of
+  15,972), and the net over the phase is +45,159,496 for +31,984 ticks, **1411.9 per tick**.
+- **Timer clock:** the net timer over the 16 s of host time is **2,822,469 per second**, 0.002 % from the
+  2,822,400 Hz of the prior art. This number doesn't depend on the rpm or the ticks per revolution. At 3600
+  ticks/rev and 33⅓ rpm the expected timer per tick is 2,822,400 · 60 / (3600 · 33⅓) = 1411.2; measured: 1411.9
+  (+0.05 %). That is the ticks-per-revolution figure below restated (timer per second / ticks per second), not a
+  second check of it.
+- **Ticks per revolution:** 31,984 ticks in 16 s is 1999.0 ticks/s, so **3598.2 ticks/rev if the motor turned at
+  exactly 33⅓** (−0.05 % from 3600). This can't separate "3600 ticks/rev and a motor 0.05 % slow" from
+  "3598 ticks/rev and an exact motor": both fit, and so would the hypothesis's 3584 (28 × 128) with a motor 0.4 %
+  fast. UNVERIFIED until a turn is counted mark to mark (M5).
+- **Spin-up:** the first note 0 press is at 19.236 s. The 0.2 s windows after it give 500, 1000, 995 and 1000
+  messages/s, and 1044, 3456, 3600 and 3609 ticks/rev at 33⅓, so the motor is at speed by about 19.6 s, roughly
+  0.4 s after the press (`--phases 19.2-19.4,19.4-19.6,19.6-19.8,19.8-20`).
+
+### By hand (2–8 s and 9–15 s)
+
+- **CC 6 advances ±1 per message by hand:** +1 for 3,536 of 3,552 deltas forward (2–8 s), −1 for 3,610 of 3,647
+  backward (9–15 s). Two-tick messages are rare (16 and 33).
+- **CC 6 is signed, the timestamp is not:** going backwards the ticks fall (−3672) while the timer still rises
+  (+15,241,219 unsigned), as a clock should. So direction comes from CC 6 alone.
+- **The timestamp only covers 5.8 ms:** the 14-bit counter spans 16384 / 2,822,400 s = **5.805 ms**. The largest
