+## The hold phase anomaly (38–41 s)
+
+While Michael held the record still with the motor running, the One did not go quiet:
+
+- **About 16 messages per second:** 50 CC 6 messages in 3 s, 16.7/s (16–18 per whole second).
+- **CC 6 dithers ±1:** −1 ×24 and +1 ×26, net +2 over 3 s. The encoder is jittering by one tick around a
+  standstill, most likely the platter (or record) moving minutely under the hand against the motor.
+- **The timestamp changes by about −1 per message:** −1 ×49 and −2 ×1, a signed net of −51 over 3 s. It does not
+  behave like a clock here at all.
+- **When −1 appears:** over the whole capture, 274 pairs have a timestamp delta of exactly −1, and **every one
+  of them came at least 5 ms (host time) after the previous pair**; none came sooner. The counter spans 5.805 ms.
+  Host time is only a rough check, though: arrival jitters by several ms (1 ms resolution, plus USB and
+  `arecordmidi` scheduling). Five real timestamps came after host gaps of 7 ms or more, for device intervals of
+  4.28–5.65 ms (at 8.104, 14.543, 37.716, 44.885 and 44.899 s; at 37.716 s the host gap is 11 ms against 4.28 ms).
